&lt;p&gt;A diligent bee flies randomly in a $6 \times 6$ honeycomb. The flight begins from the central cell. At each step, the bee moves to an adjacent cell at random, without leaving the honeycomb; thus there are $2$, $3$ or $4$ possible moves at each step depending on the bee's position.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the start of the flight, a pollen grain is placed on each cell of the leftmost column. When the bee isn't carrying a pollen grain and reaches a cell of the leftmost column containing a pollen grain, it will start to carry the pollen grain. The bee will drop the pollen grain on the first empty cell of the rightmost column it eventually reaches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's the expected number of steps until all pollen grains have been dropped in the rightmost column? &lt;br&gt;
Give your answer rounded to $6$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Consider a grid of squares, dimensioned $n \times n$, which is populated by $n^2$ ants, each occupying a single square.&lt;br&gt;
In a coordinated move, all ants decide to relocate to an adjacent square, with typically $4$ options available, unless the ant is situated on the periphery of the grid or at the corners.&lt;br&gt;
We introduce the function $f(n)$, which represents the total number of possible ways this relocation can occur, ensuring that no two ants end up on the same square and no pair of ants traverse the same edge between two squares.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
It is provided that $f(4) = 88$.&lt;br&gt;
Your task is to compute the value of $f(10)$.
